WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
<v Human Services Supervisor>I want to start with an apology. Your last three

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.400
<v Human Services Supervisor>letters were missing the requirement citation and the deadline. I

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.640
<v Human Services Supervisor>fixed them myself and never told you.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:12.386
<v Licensing Reviewer>I wondered why my letters came back changed. I thought

00:00:12.386 --> 00:00:15.782
<v Licensing Reviewer>maybe it was just style. Why didn't you say something?

00:00:16.132 --> 00:00:18.765
<v Human Services Supervisor>Honestly, I didn't want you to feel singled out. You

00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:21.397
<v Human Services Supervisor>were new, and you came from a different kind of

00:00:21.397 --> 00:00:24.030
<v Human Services Supervisor>work than most of the team. I worried you'd hear

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:24.820
<v Human Services Supervisor>it as unfair.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.911
<v Licensing Reviewer>I understand the intention. But it meant I was the

00:00:28.911 --> 00:00:32.651
<v Licensing Reviewer>only one not getting the real information. Everyone else learned

00:00:32.651 --> 00:00:36.018
<v Licensing Reviewer>the standard. I didn't, and then a provider complained.

00:00:36.368 --> 00:00:40.938
<v Human Services Supervisor>That's exactly the pattern researchers have found. In experiments, evaluators

00:00:40.938 --> 00:00:45.507
<v Human Services Supervisor>gave people from other groups the same praise, but less

00:00:45.507 --> 00:00:47.792
<v Human Services Supervisor>criticism. Criticism just quietly disappeared.

00:00:48.142 --> 00:00:51.299
<v Licensing Reviewer>And from my side, the praise starts to feel less

00:00:51.299 --> 00:00:54.456
<v Licensing Reviewer>trustworthy. If you never hear what's wrong, how do you

00:00:54.456 --> 00:00:56.350
<v Licensing Reviewer>know the good comments are real?

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.153
<v Human Services Supervisor>So here is the full message. Every correction letter needs

00:01:00.153 --> 00:01:03.607
<v Human Services Supervisor>the specific requirement and the deadline. Without both, a provider

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:07.060
<v Human Services Supervisor>may not know what to fix or by when. I

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.132
<v Human Services Supervisor>hold every reviewer to that standard.

00:01:09.482 --> 00:01:11.810
<v Licensing Reviewer>That's clear. Thank you.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.490
<v Human Services Supervisor>And I mean this part too. Providers already trust your

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.820
<v Human Services Supervisor>letters because they're clear and respectful. I'm confident you can

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.150
<v Human Services Supervisor>add these two pieces easily. Here's an example letter, if

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:22.816
<v Human Services Supervisor>it helps.

00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:26.864
<v Licensing Reviewer>That combination helps. The standard, plus knowing you think I

00:01:26.864 --> 00:01:30.562
<v Licensing Reviewer>can meet it. Can I ask one more thing? Some

00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:33.150
<v Licensing Reviewer>senior reviewers' letters miss the deadline too.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.763
<v Human Services Supervisor>Fair question. I'll pull a sample of everyone's letters this

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:40.025
<v Human Services Supervisor>week and give the same feedback to anyone with the

00:01:40.025 --> 00:01:43.287
<v Human Services Supervisor>gap. The standard isn't fair if it only applies to

00:01:43.287 --> 00:01:43.940
<v Human Services Supervisor>newer staff.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:47.190
<v Licensing Reviewer>That would mean a lot. And if you ever want

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.090
<v Licensing Reviewer>feedback on how you give feedback, I'm glad to tell

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.250
<v Licensing Reviewer>you what I notice.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.608
<v Human Services Supervisor>I'd like that. It seems only right to take the

00:01:54.608 --> 00:01:56.112
<v Human Services Supervisor>same medicine I'm handing out.
